n.Org and OptionB.Org (launching April 2017). Sheryl serves on the boards of Facebook, the Walt Disney Company, Women for Women International, ONE, and SurveyMonkey. ...more"}
{"url": "https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/5295120.Morgan_Rhodes", "name": "Morgan Rhodes", "genres": ["Young Adult", "Fantasy"], "avgRating": 4.03, "reviewsCount": 24852, "ratingsCount": 188991, "about": "Morgan Rhodes lives in Ontario, Canada. As a child, she always wanted to be a princess -- the kind that knows how to wield a sharp sword to help save both kingdoms and princes from fire-breathing dragons and dark wizards. Instead, she became a writer, which is just as good and much less dangerous. Along with writing, Morgan enjoys photography, travel, reality TV, and is an extremely picky, yet voracious reader of all kinds of books. Under another pen name, she\u2019s a national bestselling author of many paranormal novels. Falling Kingdoms is her first high fantasy."}
{"url": "https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/7680.Eugene_O_Neill", "name": "Eugene O'Neill", "birthDate": "1888-10-16 00:00:00", "deathDate": "1953-11-27 00:00:00", "genres": ["Theater"], "avgRating": 3.93, "reviewsCount": 2972, "ratingsCount": 76282, "about": "American playwright Eugene Gladstone O'Neill authored Mourning Becomes Electra in 1931 among his works; he won the Nobel Prize of 1936 for literature, and people awarded him his fourth Pulitzer Prize for Long Day's Journey into Night , produced in 1956.He won his Nobel Prize \"for the power, honesty and deep-felt emotions of his dramatic works, which embody an original concept of tragedy.\" More than any other dramatist, O'Neill introduced the dramatic realism that Russian playwright Anton Chekhov, Norwegian playwright Henrik Ibsen, and Swedish playwright August Strindberg pioneered to Americans and first used true American vernacular in his speeches. His plays involve characters, who, engaging in depraved behavior, inhabit the fringes American playwright Eugene Gladstone O'Neill authored Mourning Becomes Electra in 1931 among his works; he won the Nobel Prize of 1936 for literature, and people awarded him his fourth Pulitzer Prize for Long Day's Journey into Night , produced in 1956.He won his Nobel Prize \"for the power, honesty and deep-felt emotions of his dramatic works, which embody an original concept of tragedy.\" More than any other dramatist, O'Neill introduced the dramatic realism that Russian playwright Anton Chekhov, Norwegian playwright Henrik Ibsen, and Swedish playwright August Strindberg pioneered to Americans and first used true American vernacular in his speeches. His plays involve characters, who, engaging in depraved behavior, inhabit the fringes of society, where they struggle to maintain their hopes and aspirations but ultimately slide into disillusionment and despair. O'Neill wrote Ah, Wilderness! , his only comedy: all his other plays involve some degree of tragedy and personal pessimism. ...more"}
