{"url": "https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/16072242.Anna_Ruston", "name": "Anna Ruston", "genres": ["Biography", "Crime", "Nonfiction"], "avgRating": 4.2, "reviewsCount": 577, "ratingsCount": 6078, "about": "Anna Ruston is a pseudonym. The 44-year-old woman claims she was lured into a man\u2019s home at age 15 after a troubled upbringing. In claims that cannot be independently verified, Ms Ruston said she met her captor, Malik, at the taxi rank where she worked. Ms Ruston, now 44 with four children of her own, said the book was written partly as therapy to deal with her past but she has not yet been to police. All names have been changed and no location has been disclosed."}
{"url": "https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/934408.Bethan_Roberts", "name": "Bethan Roberts", "birthDate": "1973-08-30 00:00:00", "genres": ["Historical Fiction", "Literature & Fiction", "Gay and Lesbian"], "avgRating": 4.01, "reviewsCount": 10983, "ratingsCount": 81401, "about": "Bethan Roberts was born in Abingdon. Her first novel 'The Pools' was published in 2007 and won a Jerwood/Arvon Young Writers\u2019 Award. Her second novel 'The Good Plain Cook', published in 2008, was serialized on BBC Radio 4\u2019s Book at Bedtime and was chosen as one of Time Out\u2019s books of the year. 'My Policeman' was published by Chatto and Windus in February 2012 and was selected as that year's City Read for Brighton. Her latest novel, 'Mother Island', is longlisted for the Jerwood Fiction Uncovered Prize. She also writes short stories (in 2006 she was awarded the Olive Cook short story prize by the Society of Authors) and drama for BBC Radio 4. Bethan has worked as a television documentary researcher, writer and assistant producer, and has tau Bethan Roberts was born in Abingdon. Her first novel 'The Pools' was published in 2007 and won a Jerwood/Arvon Young Writers\u2019 Award. Her second novel 'The Good Plain Cook', published in 2008, was serialized on BBC Radio 4\u2019s Book at Bedtime and was chosen as one of Time Out\u2019s books of the year. 'My Policeman' was published by Chatto and Windus in February 2012 and was selected as that year's City Read for Brighton. Her latest novel, 'Mother Island', is longlisted for the Jerwood Fiction Uncovered Prize. She also writes short stories (in 2006 she was awarded the Olive Cook short story prize by the Society of Authors) and drama for BBC Radio 4. Bethan has worked as a television documentary researcher, writer and assistant producer, and has taught Creative Writing at Chichester University and Goldsmiths College, London. She lives in Brighton with her family. ...more"}
{"url": "https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/7125422.Jessie_Burton", "name": "Jessie Burton", "birthDate": "1982-01-01 00:00:00", "genres": ["Mystery", "Historical Fiction"], "avgRating": 3.73, "reviewsCount": 24230, "ratingsCount": 250687, "about": "Jessie Burton studied at Oxford University and the Central School of Speech and Drama, where she appeared in productions of The House of Bernarda Alba, Othello, Play and Macbeth. In April 2013 her first novel, The Miniaturist, was sold at an 11-publisher auction at the London Book Fair, and went on to sell in 29 other countries around the world. It was published by Picador in the UK and Holland in July 2014, and the USA in August 2014, with other translations to follow. Radio 4 commissioned it as their Book at Bedtime in July 2014. Her second book, The Muse, set in a dual time-frame, during the Spanish Civil War and 30 years later in 1960s London, was published in 2016. Jessie's first novel for children, The Restless Girls, will be publishe Jessie Burton studied at Oxford University and the Central School of Speech and Drama, where she appeared in productions of The House of Bernarda Alba, Othello, Play and Macbeth. In April 2013 her first novel, The Miniaturist, was sold at an 11-publisher auction at the London Book Fair, and went on to sell in 29 other countries around the world. It was published by Picador in the UK and Holland in July 2014, and the USA in August 2014, with other translations to follow. Radio 4 commissioned it as their Book at Bedtime in July 2014. Her second book, The Muse, set in a dual time-frame, during the Spanish Civil War and 30 years later in 1960s London, was published in 2016. Jessie's first novel for children, The Restless Girls, will be published in September 2018. ...more"}
