{"url": "https://www.goodreads.com/author/show/7287.Iris_Murdoch", "name": "Iris Murdoch", "birthDate": "1919-07-15 00:00:00", "deathDate": "1999-02-08 00:00:00", "genres": ["Fiction", "Philosophy"], "influences": ["William Shakespeare", "Charlotte Bront\u00eb", "Jean-Paul Sartre", "Samuel Beckett", "Simone Weil", "Sigmund Freud", "Lev Tolstoy", "Emily Bront\u00eb", "Marcel Proust", "Plato", "Henry James", "S", "Schopenhauer", "Dostoyevsky", "Thomas Hardy", "Charles Dickens"], "avgRating": 3.84, "reviewsCount": 8887, "ratingsCount": 91425, "about": "Dame Jean Iris Murdoch Irish-born British writer, university lecturer and prolific and highly professional novelist, Iris Murdoch dealt with everyday ethical or moral issues, sometimes in the light of myths. As a writer, she was a perfectionist who did not allow editors to change her text. Murdoch produced 26 novels in 40 years, the last written while she was suffering from Alzheimer disease.\"She wanted, through her novels, to reach all possible readers, in different ways and by different means: by the excitement of her story, its pace and its comedy, through its ideas and its philosophical implications, through the numinous atmosphere of her own original and created world--the world she must have glimpsed as she considered and planned her Dame Jean Iris Murdoch Irish-born British writer, university lecturer and prolific and highly professional novelist, Iris Murdoch dealt with everyday ethical or moral issues, sometimes in the light of myths. As a writer, she was a perfectionist who did not allow editors to change her text. Murdoch produced 26 novels in 40 years, the last written while she was suffering from Alzheimer disease.\"She wanted, through her novels, to reach all possible readers, in different ways and by different means: by the excitement of her story, its pace and its comedy, through its ideas and its philosophical implications, through the numinous atmosphere of her own original and created world--the world she must have glimpsed as she considered and planned her first steps in the art of fiction.\" (John Bayley in Elegy for Iris, 1998)http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Iris_Mur... ...more"}
